WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.390
2004's "Saw" was a career-making movie for
many people.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.780
Whether their roles were large or small, many
of the actors in this little-horror-movie-that-could

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.490
either had, or went on to have, surprising
careers.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.109
We want to play a game called "What happened
to the cast of Saw?"

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.320
Cary Elwes is probably best remembered for
his dashing and deadpan performance in The

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.759
Princess Bride, but he's also played a lot
of jerkish, self-centered foils, as in "Twister,"

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:33.720
and creepy antagonists, as in "Kiss the Girls."

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:40.020
"Saw" split the difference, casting him as
the selfish, jaded Dr. Gordon, who has to

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.010
spend the film growing into someone viewers
will root for.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.480
Lawrence gets put through the wringer, and
his suffering and character growth gives Elwes

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.580
the chance to deliver a juicy and intense
performance.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:57.160
You can call it over the top — and plenty
of critics did — but Elwes sells it.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.340
"I've done it!

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.970
Now show them to me!"

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.920
So what's he been doing since then?

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:11.190
For starters, he returned to the franchise
in Saw 3D, another Jigsaw victim who converted

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.190
to the killer's philosophy.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:18.750
Outside of that, he's worked steadily, usually
releasing a handful of films each year.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:24.640
With appearances in 2019's "Black Christmas''
remake, "Stranger Things" season 3, and the

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.390
acclaimed "Ghost Light," he seems to have
found a new home in horror, which tends to

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.300
be loyal to its favorite stars.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:37.310
Best of all, he also landed a role in "Mission:
Impossible 7," bringing him into one of the

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.060
most solidly entertaining franchises around.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:47.090
"Saw" co-creator Leigh Whannell's acerbic,
vulnerable Adam is a key part of what makes

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:52.780
the first "Saw" movie work, but he's arguably
even more important as the screenwriter.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.640
While Whannell's acting career has stayed
small, mostly limited to fun supporting roles

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.140
in the "Insidious" franchise and horror films
like "The Bye-Bye Man," he's become a horror

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.560
staple as a writer and director.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.579
He stayed on with "Saw" for a while, writing/co-writing
the scripts for the second and third movies,

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:16.379
and then moved on to the clever supernatural
thrills of the "Insidious" movies.

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:23.269
His directorial debut on "Insidious: Chapter
3" got an uneven reception, but with 2018's

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:27.849
sci-fi/action film "Upgrade," he started knocking
it out of the park.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.849
With the release of 2020's widely acclaimed
"The Invisible Man," the sophisticated and

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:38.239
pulse-pounding film starring Elisabeth Moss,
Whannell officially became one of the most

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:41.359
exciting horror directors to watch.

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:43.650
Danny Glover needs no introduction.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.359
With the "Lethal Weapon" series and "Predator
2" under his belt — plus roles in prestigious

00:02:48.359 --> 00:02:53.700
films like "The Color Purple," "Silverado,"
and "The Royal Tenenbaums" — he's a household

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:54.700
name.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.349
With Saw's low budget, it's impressive the
film snagged him at all.

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:04.099
He plays Tapp, a former detective who becomes
obsessed with proving that Dr. Lawrence Gordon

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:05.639
is the Jigsaw Killer.

00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:10.419
His involvement in the case gives viewers
context for Jigsaw's terrifying string of

00:03:10.419 --> 00:03:14.689
murders, and his investigation further amps
up the tension.

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:17.670
Glover's life and career have been as busy
as ever.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:23.959
He's stayed deeply involved in activism, passionately
advocating on issues ranging from labor unions

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:26.579
to international affairs and civil rights.

00:03:26.579 --> 00:03:31.609
His filmography since Saw's release has continued
to be an impressive blend of critical and

00:03:31.609 --> 00:03:35.930
commercial successes, artistic risks, and
guaranteed crowd-pleasers.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:42.120
Basically, Danny Glover can do anything, and
he's proved it by doing everything, in some

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.730
jam-packed and incredibly successful years.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:52.519
Ken Leung's doomed Detective Steven Sing might
meet a bad — but undeniably memorable — fate

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:57.079
in Saw, but Ken Leung has luckily done much,
much better in life.

00:03:57.079 --> 00:04:01.890
He was a little bit of a name before Saw,
especially given his villainous turn in "Rush

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:07.040
Hour," but he found some of his most notable
career successes after Detective Sing died

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.669
in a shotgun trap.

00:04:08.669 --> 00:04:14.510
In particular, he attracted a lot of attention
for a memorable guest-starring spot on "The

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:19.419
Sopranos" that the Washington Post pegs as
being one of the turning points in his career.

00:04:19.419 --> 00:04:24.139
He went on to play series regular Miles on
"Lost," a fan-favorite character from the

00:04:24.139 --> 00:04:28.740
back half of the show's run that many viewers
wish they'd gotten to see more of.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:35.430
Leung also had recurring roles on “The Blacklist"
and "Zero Hour" and was a series lead on medical

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.159
drama "The Night Shift."

00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:41.889
His film work hasn't been quite as high-profile,
but it's still been impressive, with roles

00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:48.449
in "Star Wars: The Force Awakens," Spike Lee's
heist thriller "Inside Man," and Noah Baumbach's

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:49.550
"The Squid and the Whale."

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:55.729
Dina Meyer has been a familiar face to genre
fans for years, appearing in everything from

00:04:55.729 --> 00:05:02.539
the satiric Starship Troopers to Star Trek:
Nemesis; she was even a series lead in 2002's

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:03.900
Birds of Prey.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.210
Playing a hard-boiled detective in Saw probably
seemed like a natural move for her career.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.250
The film wound up doing a lot for her.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:15.610
For one thing, she would go on to become a
major part of the franchise, appearing in

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:22.919
Saw II and III and staying a continuing part
of the series' lore in IV, V, and Jigsaw.

00:05:22.919 --> 00:05:28.139
Aside from the Saw series, most of Meyer's
major work has been on TV, with guest spots

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:34.930
on almost every show you could think of: NCIS:
Los Angeles, The Magicians, Criminal Minds,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:35.930
CSI, and more.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:43.670
In the first Saw film, Mike Butters plays
Paul, the man who died in Jigsaw's razor wire

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.670
trap.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:50.430
Paul might meet his grisly fate in the first
movie, but Butters still gets flashback appearances

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.620
in Saw IV and V.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.550
But he could have had an entirely different
fate in the "Saw" series.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.069
As he told Vulture,

00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:00.780
"Originally, the role I was offered was Jigsaw.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.909
Which, as you know, in the first script, really
did not have a whole lot to do, so I kind

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:09.590
of said no, which was probably a mistake!"

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:14.340
It's intriguing to play "what if" and wonder
what Butters could have done with the part.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:21.300
Paul Gutrecht gets one of the worst, hardest-to-avoid
deaths in Saw, having to walk on broken glass

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.699
holding a candle while smeared in flammable
jelly to figure out how to get an antidote

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:29.710
for poison that's already in his veins.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.620
That's a very rigged game, and it's not surprising
Mark loses.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:38.939
"Saw" was Gutrecht's final movie appearance
before he went on to pursue another career

00:06:38.939 --> 00:06:44.250
as a licensed therapist, but he did have several
guest-starring roles on TV.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:49.431
In particular, he was a favorite of showrunner
Matt Nix, who used him in Burn Notice, The

00:06:49.431 --> 00:06:52.110
Good Guys, and Complications.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:57.520
Michael Emerson, who plays fake-out villain
and reluctant Jigsaw accomplice Zep Hindle,

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.210
wound up having one of the most interesting
post-Saw careers of the whole cast.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:07.449
While he appeared in a few more movies, Emerson's
strongest work has been on TV, where he's

00:07:07.449 --> 00:07:12.720
found several awesome, intriguing roles that
really showcase his talent.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:19.419
Even before Saw, Emerson had an Emmy Award-winning
guest star run on the legal drama The Practice,

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:22.629
but it was only after Saw that his career
really took off.

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:29.310
He was cast as Ben Linus on Lost, and the
part catapulted him to national recognition.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:33.990
His work as Ben won him another Emmy, this
time for Outstanding Supporting Actor in a

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.530
Drama Series.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:41.240
Emerson undeniably made the most of his Lost
success, going on to star in the thought-provoking

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:46.460
sci-fi thriller series Person of Interest,
the historical miniseries The Name of the

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:50.050
Rose, and the supernatural horror drama Evil.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.009
Truly a geek icon.

00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:58.599
Benito Martinez's Brett has one of the safest
roles in Saw: He plays Lawrence Gordon's lawyer,

00:07:58.599 --> 00:08:02.409
and he gets in and out without attracting
any of Jigsaw's ire.

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:04.879
He even gives solid legal advice.

00:08:04.879 --> 00:08:10.580
And once Brett escaped, Martinez continued
an impressive career that's made him one of

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.240
TV's low-key MVPs.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:18.919
He was already starring on The Shield, where
he played the calculatingly ambitious David

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:23.990
Aceveda, and he continued in that role until
the show ended in 2008.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.169
Drawing off his great work there, he's become
a kind of sleeper hit on cable shows and some

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:34.680
of the darker network dramas, with prominent
roles on Sons of Anarchy, House of Cards,

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:40.160
American Crime, The Blacklist, How to Get
Away with Murder, and 13 Reasons Why.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.600
He hasn't worked on as many movies, but he's
still been a part of some great projects,

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.370
including Queen &amp; Slim, Beyond the Lights,
and Million Dollar Baby.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.280
Shawnee Smith's Amanda is one of the most
memorable characters in Saw, even with very

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:01.410
limited screen time: Her desperation is vivid,
and she's absolutely mesmerizing as someone

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.280
who has fallen under Jigsaw's spell.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:09.220
The series knew that her character had promise
— and that Smith, who had an established

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:12.760
acting career, especially on TV, was a good
bet.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:18.660
She became a huge part of the franchise, with
major roles in Saw II and III.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.180
"Game over."

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.220
"I'll f---ing kill you!"

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:28.791
Her future Saw appearances, especially with
her significantly expanded part, helped make

00:09:28.791 --> 00:09:34.650
Smith into a little bit of a horror name:
It led to her hosting the reality show Scream

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:40.410
Queens in 2008, where she took young actresses
through a competition to win a role in Saw

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:41.500
VI.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:47.340
But unlike a lot of Saw actors, she didn't
completely redirect her career to horror.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:52.330
With previous work on shows like Becker, she
still had one foot in comedy, and her most

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:57.110
significant post-Saw work has come on the
FX sitcom Anger Management.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:03.310
As the menaced and terrified daughter in Saw,
Makenzie Vega doesn't necessarily have much

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.790
to do... but to be fair, she was around ten
when the movie was filmed.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:13.330
In the wake of the film, she went on to grow
up and develop as an actor, eventually demonstrating

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.870
some pretty strong dramatic chops.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:21.180
She really made a splash on the hit CBS show
The Good Wife, where she played series regular

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.530
Grace, the opinionated and independent daughter
of lead Alicia Florrick.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.940
Monica Potter imbues Alison Gordon with a
sense of steely poise even though she spends

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.040
most of the movie fearing for her and her
daughter's lives.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:41.350
A few years after Saw, she would play another
memorable horror wife and mother, this time

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:45.430
in the 2009 remake of The Last House on the
Left.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.950
Most of Potter's career, however, has been
in television.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:53.690
"Saw" overlapped with her time as a series
regular on Boston Legal, where she played

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:55.820
lawyer Lori Colson.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:11:01.700
After a few years hopping around between intermittent
projects, including a regular role on TNT's

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:07.420
short-lived Trust Me, she found spectacular
success with NBC's Parenthood, which gave

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.270
her a chance to really shine.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:15.670
Her nuanced performance as Kristina Braverman
won her a Critics Choice Award in 2013.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.950
Since the show wrapped, she's continued to
work in TV, including a lead role on Wisdom

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:21.950
of the Crowd.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:28.570
Poor Jeff Ridenhour narrowly misses one of
the most humiliating deaths in the franchise.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:34.000
Jigsaw uses him as a test subject for a trap
— it's not even about him! — and the detectives

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.120
who discover him decide to hang back and see
what Jigsaw will do with him before they step

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.120
in.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.260
Not exactly great police work.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:47.020
Ned Bellamy, the actor who played him, has
gotten better treatment as he's developed

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:49.140
a career as a character actor.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:54.830
As a guest star, he feels like a seamless
part of the ensemble on everything from Scrubs

00:11:54.830 --> 00:12:01.440
to Justified to Treme, and he's had a pleasantly
varied movie career with roles in Django Unchained

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.440
and Twilight.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:09.360
Alexandra Chun plays Carla, the medical student
Lawrence has an ill-advised affair with.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:13.780
After Saw, she went on to have a strong career
that's only getting better over time.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:19.750
Her biggest movie role so far has been as
the lead in 2013's Innocent Blood, a well-regarded

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.050
indie thriller.

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:25.970
And she's done a lot of TV guest star work,
appearing on beloved shows like House, The

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:29.570
Event, The Magicians, and Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:36.330
Recently, she's gotten more substantial recurring
roles on both Twitch's semi-interactive Artificial

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:39.230
and the Netflix ballet drama Tiny Pretty Things.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:42.050
"I want to play a game."

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:47.710
Even before Saw, Tobin Bell had built an impressive
body of work: He was someone you could spot

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:52.510
in everything from Tootsie to Goodfellas,
in part because he appreciated any chance

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:53.820
to get on set.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:54.940
As he told Movieline,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:13:01.140
"I viewed it as an opportunity to make $150
a day doing something that I cared a great

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.540
deal about, where I could learn."

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:09.130
Saw changed all that, splitting his career
into "before" and "after" stages.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:14.210
Speaking with the Orlando Sentinel, he called
the movie "a great blessing," and he starred

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:19.650
in multiple Saw sequels, appearing in every
installment even after his character's death

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:20.920
in Saw III.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.670
Understandably, he's now best known as a horror
actor, so he's also appeared in other horror

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:31.210
projects like 12 Feet Deep, Belzebuth, and
Shudder's Creepshow.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:35.900
But he still occasionally gets to stretch
himself, including with voice work on CW's

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:36.900
The Flash.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:41.130
"You have no idea what's about to begin."

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:46.210
With his role in the franchise wrapping up,
he's eager to see if his increased star power

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:51.480
can translate to different kinds of roles,
ones very far from John Kramer.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.920
We hope he'll never leave horror behind completely,
but after years of masterfully orchestrating

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:01.510
terror for audiences everywhere, he certainly
deserves a change of pace.

